# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
# The audit in antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197 found exactly that drift
# across the constellation, which is why this file exists.
#
# What this repo actually produces on a pull request, verified against the
# check-suite of the latest pull_request run (suite 93756775747) and against
# the check-runs on main's HEAD (fbb69d3), not read off the YAML:
#
#   test-ubuntu-latest   github-actions   success
#   test-macos-latest    github-actions   success
#
# That is the whole of it. `ci.yml` sets `name: test-${{ matrix.os }}`, so the
# legs report under a flat `test-<os>` name rather than the `test / <os>`
# job-matrix spelling other repos in the constellation use — the names below
# are the literal strings GitHub reported, copied, not reconstructed.
#
# `release.yml` also defines a job called `test`, and a `publish to crates.io`
# job after it. Neither is declared and neither may ever be: that workflow
# triggers only on `push: tags: ['v*']`, so on a pull request it never reports
# at all. A required check that cannot report is not a gate, it is a permanent
# block on merging anything, and with enforce_admins on there is no way round
# it from the UI.
#
# `ci-ok` is the aggregate gate, added in the same change as this file. It is
# `if: always()` and `needs: [test]`, and it fails unless the matrix concluded
# exactly `success` — so failed, cancelled and skipped all come out red. It
# exists because the two matrix legs below are a list that has to be kept in
# step with `ci.yml` by hand: add `windows-latest` to the matrix tomorrow and
# the new leg gates nothing, because nobody remembered to come back here. A
# job that `needs:` the whole matrix covers every leg the matrix ever grows,
# including ones that do not exist yet.
#
# The two legs are ALSO declared, deliberately, rather than being replaced by
# `ci-ok`. An aggregate is a claim about other jobs, and the failure this sweep
# is about is a green check that stands in for work that did not happen; if
# `ci-ok` were ever weakened into passing on a skipped matrix, requiring it
# alone would hand back the same fake coverage in a new shape. Requiring the
# legs as well means the real compile-and-test work must have reported success
# under its own name before anything merges, whatever `ci-ok` believes.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
	required = test-ubuntu-latest
	required = test-macos-latest
